  "content": {
    "id": "p15-q2-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "2",
    "prompt": "Solve each of the following equations, checking that the values obtained are valid solutions.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p15-q2-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$\\log_2 x+\\log_2(x-2)=3$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=4$",
          "worked": "The original logarithms require $x>2$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_2[x(x-2)]&=3\\\\x(x-2)&=2^3\\\\x^2-2x-8&=0\\\\(x-4)(x+2)&=0\\\\x&=4\\text{ or }-2\\end{align*}$$\n\nReject $x=-2$ because $x>2$. For $x=4$, $\\log_2 4+\\log_2 2=2+1=3$. Therefore, $x=4$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 49
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-q2-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$\\log_3 x+\\log_3(x-6)=3$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=9$",
          "worked": "The original logarithms require $x>6$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_3[x(x-6)]&=3\\\\x(x-6)&=3^3\\\\x^2-6x-27&=0\\\\(x-9)(x+3)&=0\\\\x&=9\\text{ or }-3\\end{align*}$$\n\nReject $x=-3$ because $x>6$. For $x=9$, $\\log_3 9+\\log_3 3=2+1=3$. Therefore, $x=9$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 49
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-q2-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$\\log_6(x+2)+\\log_6(x-3)=2$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=7$",
          "worked": "The original logarithms require $x>3$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_6[(x+2)(x-3)]&=2\\\\(x+2)(x-3)&=6^2\\\\x^2-x-6&=36\\\\x^2-x-42&=0\\\\(x-7)(x+6)&=0\\\\x&=7\\text{ or }-6\\end{align*}$$\n\nReject $x=-6$ because $x>3$. For $x=7$, both arguments are positive and $\\log_6 9+\\log_6 4=\\log_6 36=2$. Therefore, $x=7$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 55
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-q2-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$\\log_3 x+\\log_3(x-2)=\\log_3(x+10)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=5$",
          "worked": "The original logarithms require $x>2$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_3[x(x-2)]&=\\log_3(x+10)\\\\x(x-2)&=x+10\\\\x^2-2x&=x+10\\\\x^2-3x-10&=0\\\\(x-5)(x+2)&=0\\\\x&=5\\text{ or }-2\\end{align*}$$\n\nReject $x=-2$ because $x>2$. For $x=5$, both sides equal $\\log_3 15$, with all original arguments positive. Therefore, $x=5$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 55
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-q2-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$\\log_2(2x)+\\log_2(x-1)=\\log_2(x^2+3)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=3$",
          "worked": "The original logarithms require $x>1$; $x^2+3$ is always positive.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_2[2x(x-1)]&=\\log_2(x^2+3)\\\\2x(x-1)&=x^2+3\\\\2x^2-2x&=x^2+3\\\\x^2-2x-3&=0\\\\(x+1)(x-3)&=0\\\\x&=-1\\text{ or }3\\end{align*}$$\n\nReject $x=-1$ because $x>1$. For $x=3$, both sides equal $\\log_2 12$, with all original arguments positive. Therefore, $x=3$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 55
      }
    ]
  },
